# Line endings, and why this file exists.
#
# The impairment crate's acceptance criterion is a byte comparison between a
# committed decision-log fixture and a log rendered at run time. The renderer
# emits U+000A and nothing else, on every platform. Without the rule below, a
# clone made with `core.autocrlf = true` rewrites the fixture's line endings on
# checkout, and the comparison is then a test of the developer's git config.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` normalises every text file to LF in the repository and
# checks it out as LF everywhere, so a Windows working tree and a Linux one hold
# the same bytes.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The fixtures are exempted from conversion entirely rather than merely pinned
# to LF. A fixture is the phase's product, not source code: nothing may rewrite
# a byte of it, in either direction, for any reason. `-text` disables the clean
# and smudge filters outright, which is a stronger statement than "convert it to
# LF" and does not depend on git's text/binary heuristic agreeing with us.
#
# This is deliberately belt and braces with the comparison itself, which strips
# a trailing carriage return from both sides before comparing. Either mechanism
# alone would do on a fresh clone; together they mean a checkout that predates
# this file, or a developer who overrode it, still cannot turn the acceptance
# criterion into a platform-dependent coin flip.
crates/quinn-netem/tests/fixtures/*.log -text
